.TL
Life expectancy in the states, 1977
.AU
A. Student
.AI
Bell Laboratories
Murray Hill, New Jersey
.AB
A first look at life expectancy in the 50 states, with the state.x77
data that comes with R, as it came with S.
Every number, listing and figure in this report was computed by R
while the report was being formatted, by knitroff.
.AE
.SR lo min(state.x77[, "Life Exp"])
.SR hi max(state.x77[, "Life Exp"])
.SR mn round(mean(state.x77[, "Life Exp"]), 2)
.SR sd round(sd(state.x77[, "Life Exp"]), 2)
.NH
The data
.PP
In 1977 life expectancy ranged from \*[lo] to \*[hi] years,
with a mean of \*[mn] years and a standard deviation of \*[sd].
The summaries and a stem-and-leaf display come straight from R:
.SS summaries
life <- state.x77[, "Life Exp"]   # years, for 1969-71
mean(life); median(life)
sd(life)
stem(life)
.SE
.PP
The standard deviation is the square root of the sample variance
.EQ
s sup 2 ~=~ 1 over {n - 1} sum from i=1 to n ( x sub i - x bar ) sup 2
.EN
which is what \f(CRvar\fP computes.
.NH
Life expectancy and income
.PP
Richer states tend to live longer, up to a point.
A scatter plot with Cleveland's lowess smooth shows the shape:
.SS scatter, fig.cap="Life expectancy against income, with a lowess smooth.", fig.height=4
income <- state.x77[, "Income"]
plot(income, life, xlab = "Income", ylab = "Life expectancy", pch = 20)
lines(lowess(income, life))   # Cleveland's smoother, 1979
.SE
.PP
The correlation is only `r round(cor(income, life), 2)`: the smooth rises with income,
then levels off, and one rich, outlying state pulls its right end down.
.NH
The longest and shortest lives
.SS extremes, echo=FALSE
o <- order(life)
knitroff::roff_table(data.frame(State = state.name[o], Income = income[o], "Life expectancy" = life[o],
                                check.names = FALSE)[c(1:3, 48:50), ],
                     caption = "The three shortest and three longest lives")
.SE
